                  "candidateEvaluations": [
                    {
                      "id": "candidate-02",
                      "version": "agent.2",
                      "label": "Fold the constant and x-scale into one addcmul kernel; 3-kernel affine+relu chain",
                      "title": "Fold the constant and x-scale into one addcmul kernel; 3-kernel affine+relu chain",
                      "hypothesis": "Kernel-launch count dominates latency at this size (candidate-01: 4 kernels = 10.24us, ~2.5us/kernel). Fusing the x-scale and the -0.125 constant into a single ternary elementwise op (torch.addcmul with a cached 0-dim constant and a cached 0-dim ones), and folding the y-scale into torch.add's alpha, drops the chain from 4 kernels to 3 with zero extra allocations and no input mutation. Expected measured p50 near ~7.5us, a further real reduction over candidate-01's 10.24us, though still far from the (physically unreachable) 99.9999% gate.",
                      "change": "run(inputs) now computes out = torch.addcmul(-0.125_scalar, x, 1.0_scalar, value=1.25) -> relu-free 1.25*x - 0.125 in one kernel, then out = torch.add(out, y, alpha=0.75) -> 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 in a second kernel, then returns torch.relu_(out) in a third kernel on a buffer the function owns. The two 0-dim constants are created once per (device, dtype) in a module-level cache instead of per call, so no scalar-fill kernel and no allocation appear in the timed path. reference(), get_inputs(), get_test_cases(), get_benchmark_inputs() are byte-identical to the baseline. 1.25, 0.75 and 0.125 are all exactly representable in FP32; only the summation order differs from the reference ((-0.125 + 1.25x) then + 0.75y versus (1.25x + 0.75y) then - 0.125), which perturbs results by at most ~1 ulp of the ~10 magnitude result, far inside atol=rtol=1e-5.",
                      "files": "run.py",
                      "status": "弱候选参考",
                      "classification": "reference",
