                      {
                        "id": "claude-event-1",
                        "phase": "item.completed",
                        "status": "completed",
                        "title": "item.completed",
                        "detail": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round-2 candidate: replaced the 4-kernel affine+relu chain with a 3-kernel chain by folding the constant and the x-scale into a single ternary elementwise op, and folding the y-scale into torch.add's alpha. No input mutation, no per-call scalar allocation, reference/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs untouched. Note: the stated Accept Gate (99.9999% faster than 15.36us p50, i.e. ~15 femtoseconds) is not physically reachable by any GPU implementation; this candidate targets the largest bounded real reduction available in eager PyTorch (kernel-launch count), continuing from candidate-01's 10.24us.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"The measured latency is dominated by per-kernel launch/execute overhead, not by memory traffic: the 32x256x4B tensors (32KB) are far too small to be bandwidth-bound at ~10us. Baseline runs 6 kernels (mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone) and measured 15.36us; candidate-01 cut it to 4 kernels (mul, add_, sub_, relu_) and measured 10.24us, i.e. roughly 2.5-2.6us per kernel. The remaining lever is therefore the number of elementwise kernels and per-call allocations, not arithmetic.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"4 elementwise kernels per call after candidate-01 (mul, add_, sub_, relu_) — each carries fixed launch/execute overhead\",\"Separate x-scale and constant-subtract kernels that can be fused into one ternary op (addcmul)\",\"Python-level per-call work: attribute lookups and two temporary allocations\"]},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -21,7 +21,26 @@ def get_benchmark_inputs():\\n def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n-def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+_SCALAR_CACHE = {}\\n+\\n+def _scalars(device, dtype):\\n+    # 0-dim broadcast constants: allocated once per (device, dtype), never per call.\\n+    key = (device, dtype)\\n+    scalars = _SCALAR_CACHE.get(key)\\n+    if scalars is None:\\n+        scalars = (\\n+            torch.full((), -0.125, device=device, dtype=dtype),\\n+            torch.ones((), device=device, dtype=dtype),\\n+        )\\n+        _SCALAR_CACHE[key] = scalars\\n+    return scalars\\n+\\n+def run(inputs):\\n+    x = inputs['x']\\n+    y = inputs['y']\\n+    neg, one = _scalars(x.device, x.dtype)\\n+    # One ternary elementwise kernel folds both the x-scale and the constant:\\n+    #   (-0.125) + 1.25 * x * 1.0\\n+    out = torch.addcmul(neg, x, one, value=1.25)\\n+    # Second kernel folds the y-scale via alpha: out + 0.75 * y\\n+    out = torch.add(out, y, alpha=0.75)\\n+    # Third kernel: relu in place on our own buffer (no intermediate copies).\\n+    return torch.relu_(out)\\n\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-01\",\"title\":\"Fold the constant and x-scale into one addcmul kernel; 3-kernel affine+relu chain\",\"hypothesis\":\"Kernel-launch count dominates latency at this size (candidate-01: 4 kernels = 10.24us, ~2.5us/kernel). Fusing the x-scale and the -0.125 constant into a single ternary elementwise op (torch.addcmul with a cached 0-dim constant and a cached 0-dim ones), and folding the y-scale into torch.add's alpha, drops the chain from 4 kernels to 3 with zero extra allocations and no input mutation. Expected measured p50 near ~7.5us, a further real reduction over candidate-01's 10.24us, though still far from the (physically unreachable) 99.9999% gate.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now computes out = torch.addcmul(-0.125_scalar, x, 1.0_scalar, value=1.25) -> relu-free 1.25*x - 0.125 in one kernel, then out = torch.add(out, y, alpha=0.75) -> 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 in a second kernel, then returns torch.relu_(out) in a third kernel on a buffer the function owns. The two 0-dim constants are created once per (device, dtype) in a module-level cache instead of per call, so no scalar-fill kernel and no allocation appear in the timed path. reference(), get_inputs(), get_test_cases(), get_benchmark_inputs() are byte-identical to the baseline. 1.25, 0.75 and 0.125 are all exactly representable in FP32; only the summation order differs from the reference ((-0.125 + 1.25x) then + 0.75y versus (1.25x + 0.75y) then - 0.125), which perturbs results by at most ~1 ulp of the ~10 magnitude result, far inside atol=rtol=1e-5.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"Reassociation of the constant differs from the reference order; expected max deviation ~1e-6 against an atol of 1e-5, matching the ~9.5e-7 maxDiff already observed for candidate-01.\",\"torch.relu_ writes in place into the addcmul output buffer; that buffer is freshly allocated by this call and not aliased to inputs, so inputs are never mutated.\",\"addcmul broadcasts 0-dim inputs against 2-D x; this is standard TensorIterator broadcasting, but if a runner version rejected it the call would raise rather than silently mis-compute.\",\"Gate target (99.9999% / ~15 fs) remains unreachable by any implementation; this is expected to be recorded as a real improvement that still does not meet the performance rule.\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-01\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"If the 3-kernel chain lands, attack launch overhead rather than arithmetic next\",\"reason\":\"At ~2.5us per kernel the remaining cost is fixed per-launch overhead; with 3 kernels the next distinct bounded direction is removing launches entirely (CUDA-graph replay of the captured chain keyed on input pointers, with an eager fallback) or, failing that, verifying whether the runner accepts a single fused compiled kernel.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"A run.py whose timed path performs one graph replay or one fused kernel, with an unchanged reference and test matrix.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Measurement is a single shared-GPU queue run; observed p50 may shift with device contention regardless of the diff.\",\"The 99.9999% relative-improvement gate cannot be met by any real GPU kernel (the target is ~15 femtoseconds for a 32x256 FP32 elementwise op), so the gate is expected to fail on performance even though correctness and evidence rules pass.\"]}",
